Tense, terrific and emotionally charged, XCOM: Enemy Unknown is unlike any game of recent times.
Part turn-based shooter, part crisis management simulator, XCOM follows on from the 1994 classic, XCOM: UFO Defence.
However, developers Firaxis have managed to successfully evolve the gameplay while staying respectful to its roots.
Work as a team: Whether your team comes back alive is entirely up to you
In many ways, it's still the XCOM fans know and love. Although large squads have been replaced by a small, tight-knit group of hardened soldiers, whether they come back alive is entirely up to you.
Furthermore, you'll be faced by various moral dilemmas - choosing who to help and who to ignore - and what to research - whether that be conducting an alien autopsy or creating new weapons.
Combat is an edge-of-the-seat affair - made all the more immersive by the fantastic soundtrack.
Each team takes turns to meander around the level. The quality of cover is indicated by a shield - if full then you're safe, if half full then expect to be obliterated given time.
Although the Unreal 3 engine barely breaks a sweat rendering characters and environments, there's a satisfyingly brutal element to ongoing combat.
Aliens explode into clouds of green ooze, but more disturbingly, the most gruesome deaths are saved for your squad members.
At the start, you squad may primarily consist of rookies - but over time, they will develop into hardened killing machines.
It's often hard to watch a seasoned veteran fall during a firefight - made worse given the fact that once they die, they can't be played again.
Battles are an emotionally-charged affair - on one hand you try to keep your best soldiers alive, on the other - you have to do what is best for the squad as a whole.
Suit up: You can customise your characters' abilities, weapons and appearance
Away from the battlefield and the tension doesn't let up, either. Here, your primary objective is to save the planet with a very limited supply of resources.
Cash is sparse and engineers and scientists are always demanding for new facilities to be built.
You may turn to the 'Grey Market' for quick funds, but this may lead to you selling vital alien equipment retrieved from battle.
There's always that little bit too much going on to suggest that you're fully in control. If you ignore some countries, they will withdrawal from the XCOM project - taking with them valuable resources.
Although daunting at the start, throughout the game you'll find decisions easier to make - having developed a tough outer shell from the serious consequences of your previous actions.
You'll often have to juggle calming your squad after their leader has been killed, while trying to the council satisfied by completing near-{g}imp{g}ossible{r} missions{g}.{g}
