Nearly a week after the catastrophic bushfires, communities across Victoria are struggling to come to terms with their losses and start planning for the future.
In the east of the state, the small township of Traralgon South had a narrow escape from the inferno when the winds suddenly changed direction.
Neighbours down the road at Callignee and Koornalla weren't so lucky. Sixteen people are believed to have died there.
Last night, the weekly get-together of the local Traralgon South Cricket Club was opened to everyone from the area to come to its rooms for a beer and to have a chat.
Beef and sheep farmer John Black says the night was a way to help survivors get back to reality and start life again.
"It's just to get the people to tell their stories, to say what they think and express themselves," Mr Black said.
"A lot of them are finding it hard to come out of their shells.
"For the people that have survived, they feel lucky and guilty that we have been untouched while people have lost husbands, wives and stock a kilometre away."
Stephen and Christine Lynch followed their fire action plan and stayed with their house.
It is now one of the few left standing in Callignee, but they say it was more a case of the house being spared than them saving it.
They lost a beloved horse and half of their cows.
"We were on the south-west end of the house at the time, trying to put out the ember attack, and it just came with such a ferocity that it came across our trees," said Mr Lynch, who suffered burns to his arms and neck.
"We had to run probably 25 metres to get to the centre of the house and everything caught on fire including my hair, and any exposed skin just peeled off. So, that's how hot it was."
