If not for a few retirement and luxury condominium developments, new condominium demand in Broward County would be virtually non-existent.
Demand for new condominiums has declined throughout the 1980s and into the 1990s. Statistics from Appraisal Real Estate and Economics Associates show that in the tri-county area, a total of 364 condominiums were built and sold in February. That compares with 450 that were built and sold in February a year earlier, or a decline of 19 percent.
In February 1983, which was the first year of recovery after the 1982 recession, 879 condominiums were built and sold.
A large part of the market has disappeared because first-time home buyers no longer are interested in suburban condominiums. Instead, people who need apartments opt for the thousands of rentals that have been developed in South Florida since 1985.
Those who remain in the condominium development business continue to be the historical leaders in the industry. In Broward, the top five developers are Cenvill, Lennar, Palm Aire, Minto and Oriole Homes. In Palm Beach County, the top five developers are Oriole Homes, Minto, Yale Properties, Arvida and Lennar.
The strongest demand in South Florida is at waterfront locations along the ocean or Intracoastal Waterway in Dade County. Sales have not declined significantly in Dade because new buildings in areas such as Surfside and Miami Beach continue to attract buyers.
However, in Broward and Palm Beach counties, waterfront developments are few and far between. The waterfront markets in both counties could even be considered under-built.
The industry leaders in Broward and Palm Beach counties cater primarily to the moderately priced markets. This is typified by Minto and Oriole Homes, which continue to capture the majority of the remaining retirement demand in South Florida.
---- David Dabby is senior vice president at AREEA (Appraisal Real Estate and Economics Associates) in Miami. Information for his column comes from AREEA reports and courthouse records.
