The Government 'is complicit in a system that is undermining productivity and perpetuating the gender pay gap', according to a select committee report.
The Women and Equalities Select Committee found the underlying causes of the gap - which means that women will earn 80p for every £1 earned by men - to be complex.
Key factors were the part-time 'pay penalty', women's disproportionate responsibility for childcare and other forms of unpaid care, and the concentration of women in low-paid sectors such as care, retail and cleaning.
Complex issue: Women are concentrated in 'highly feminised' low-paid sectors like care work
The gender pay gap measures the difference between the hourly pay of the average man and the average woman, rather than measuring the difference in what they are paid for doing the same job.
While the overall pay gap remains at around 19.2 per cent, for women aged 50 to 59 it widens to 27 per cent.
And while women are clearly suffering from this inequality, so are the country's finances. The under-utilisation of women��s skills costs the UK economy an estimated 2 per cent of GDP, which equates to around £36billion.
The committee found that while the Government'recognises the value of modernising the workplace', it is still not taking action to ensure flexible working is offered to all employees, particularly those in lower-paid sectors.
